# E0200: a zero-arg READER takes a shared receiver lock for its duration, like
# any other call, so it is refused while an exclusive reference is live.
#
# `length` and `capacity` are materialised as plain u64 members -- they emit as
# a struct field read rather than a call -- so they resolve to their RETURN type
# and every mechanism keyed on "is this a method?" used to skip them. The result
# was that `xs.length` was ACCEPTED inside an exclusively-pinned walk while
# `xs.contains item: x` beside it was refused, though both are readers.
#
# Under the readonly `iterate` this is legal (a shared pin coexists with a
# shared read) -- see map_get_while_iterate. It is `iterateMut`, which pins
# exclusively, that must refuse it.
main: function is {
    xs: (List i64)
    xs.append from: 1
    n: 0.u64
    c: 0.u64
    with it: xs.iterateMut do for x: it loop {
        n = n + xs.length
        c = c + xs.capacity
    }
}
